{"name":"Timber Beam","hex":"#a0855c","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"timber","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Trees in a forest regarded as a source of wood.","Wood that has been pre-cut and is ready for use in construction."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Used by loggers to warn others that a tree being felled is falling.","A cry used when anything is falling over."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/timber"},{"term":"beam","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any large piece of timber or iron long in proportion to its thickness, and prepared for use.","One of the principal horizontal structural members, usually of steel, timber, or concrete, of a building."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To emit beams of light; to shine; to radiate.","To smile broadly or especially cheerfully."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beam"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lumber","blocks":[{"text":"Lumber, also called timber or fine wood in the United Kingdom, Australia, and New Zealand, is wood that has been processed into uniform and useful sizes, including beams and planks or boards. Lumber is mainly used for construction framing, as well as finishing. Lumber has many uses beyond home building. In some parts of the world, including the United States and Canada, the term timber refers specifically to unprocessed wood fiber, such as cut logs or standing trees that have yet to be cut."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Wood_from_victoria_mountain_ash.jpg/330px-Wood_from_victoria_mountain_ash.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lumber"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Bellingham%2C_Washington%2C_harbor%2C_filled_with_logs%2C_1972.jpg/500px-Bellingham%2C_Washington%2C_harbor%2C_filled_with_logs%2C_1972.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm